# Gotchas

Real mechanics of how this project's Celery setup is wired — not a
general Celery tutorial — worth knowing before you hit the same wall.

## A new `tasks/` module doesn't run itself — `worker.py` has to import it

Pointing `celery -A {{ package_name }}.worker.celery_app worker` at
`worker.py` only gets the worker process the `celery_app` instance.
Celery doesn't scan `tasks/` on its own; a task only gets *registered*
when the module defining it is *imported* somewhere in the process,
because `@celery_app.task` does its registration as a side effect of
that module executing. `tasks/example.py` only becomes visible to the
worker because `worker.py` ends with:

```python
from {{ package_name }}.tasks import example  # noqa: E402,F401
```

That import can't move to the top of `worker.py`, and it's not there by
accident. `tasks/example.py` does `from {{ package_name }}.worker import
celery_app` — it needs the `celery_app` instance to decorate `add` with
`@celery_app.task`. So `worker.py` can't import `tasks/example` before
`celery_app` is defined without hitting a circular import (`worker.py`
importing `tasks/example.py` importing `worker.py`, which isn't
finished defining `celery_app` yet). Building `celery_app` first, then
importing the task modules that depend on it, is the only way to break
that cycle — which is why the import sits at the *bottom* of the file,
after a `# noqa: E402,F401` (import-not-at-top and unused-import,
both intentional here).

**The consequence for you**: every new module you add under `tasks/`
needs its own import line added to the bottom of `worker.py`, right
next to the `tasks.example` one. Skip it and the task function still
imports and runs fine anywhere else in your code — `add.delay(...)`
even successfully publishes a message to the broker — but the worker
process never registers it, so the message just sits unconsumed and the
task silently never executes. There's no error; it looks like a hang.

{% if framework == "fastapi" %}
## `.delay()`/`.apply_async()` are sync calls — don't `await` them

Celery's client API (`.delay()`, `.apply_async()`, `AsyncResult(...)`)
is synchronous by design — under the hood it's a blocking publish to
{{ broker }}, not a coroutine. `main.py`'s `enqueue_add` is a plain
`def`, not `async def`, even though the rest of this FastAPI app favors
`async def` handlers the moment they do I/O (see the `fastapi` skill).
Don't "fix" this by making it `async def` and `await add.delay(a, b)` —
`.delay()` doesn't return an awaitable, so that's a `TypeError` at
request time, not a style improvement. If you need to call it from code
that's already inside an `async def`, call it as-is (unawaited); Celery
has no async client.
{% endif %}

## Forgetting `PYTHONPATH=src` breaks the worker, not the API

The API process finds `{{ package_name }}` because it's installed
editable into the project's virtualenv. The `celery` CLI's `-A` flag
does a plain module import (`{{ package_name }}.worker`) from whatever's
on `PYTHONPATH`, and this project's source lives under `src/` rather
than the repo root — so running
`uv run celery -A {{ package_name }}.worker.celery_app worker` without
`PYTHONPATH=src` fails with `ModuleNotFoundError:
No module named '{{ package_name }}'` even though the API starts fine.
Always use the full command from the README:

```
PYTHONPATH=src uv run celery -A {{ package_name }}.worker.celery_app worker --loglevel=info
```

{% if broker == "rabbitmq" %}
## The `rpc://` result backend only delivers to the process that enqueued the task

This project configures `backend="rpc://"` when the broker is
RabbitMQ (see `worker.py`) instead of pointing the backend at Redis.
`rpc://` implements results as an AMQP reply sent back to the exact
connection that called `.delay()`/`.apply_async()` — it is not a shared
store any process can poll later. Enqueuing in one process (e.g. a
request handler) and then trying to fetch
`AsyncResult(task_id, app=celery_app).result` from a *different*,
unrelated process (a separate script, a different API worker) is not a
supported pattern with `rpc://` — you'll get a result that never
arrives rather than a clear error. If you need results that any process
can read back later (a status-polling endpoint hit by a different
request than the one that enqueued the task, for example), point the
result backend at Redis instead, the way the `broker == "redis"` branch
of `worker.py` already does for both broker and backend.
{% endif %}
